ays” packets across\n     all active sockets while keeping cost tiny.\n   - Exactly one bpf_sk_select_reuseport call; clamp active to [1,128].\n*/\nSEC(\"sk_reuseport/selector\")\nenum sk_action agent_udp_selector(struct sk_reuseport_md *reuse)\n{\n    __u32 key = 0;\n    struct agent_state *st = bpf_map_lookup_elem(&pebble_agent_state, &key);\n    if (!st)\n        return SK_PASS;\n\n    __u32 active = st->active;\n    if (active == 0)\n        return SK_PASS;\n    if (active > 128)\n        active = 128;\n\n    // Add per-packet jitter, then whiten (Murmur3 fmix32).\n    __u32 h = reuse->hash ^ bpf_get_prandom_u32();\n    h ^= h >> 16;\n    h *= 0x85ebca6b;\n    h ^= h >> 13;\n    h *= 0xc2b2ae35;\n    h ^= h >> 16;\n\n    __u32 slot;\n    if ((active & (active - 1)) == 0) {\n        slot = h & (active - 1);\n    } else {\n        __u64 prod = (__u64)h * active;\n        slot = (__u32)(prod >> 32);\n    }\n\n    bpf_sk_select_reuseport(reuse, &pebble_udp_targets, &slot, 0);\n    return SK_PASS;\n}\n// EVOLVE-BLOCK-END\n\nchar _license[] SEC(\"license\") = \"GPL\";\n", "language": "cpp", "parent_id": "21221b76-4a83-4fdc-b90d-a3a7776dc577", "generation": 3, "timestamp": 1763056117.637401, "iteration_found": 9, "metrics": {"policy": "agent", "rates": [50000, 60000, 80000, 100000, 140000], "load_p99_curve": [[50000.0, 2.214], [60000.0, 2.739], [80000.0, 3.31], [100000.0, 4.562], [140000.0, 15.227]], "load_p99_stddev_curve": [[50000.0, 0.0], [60000.0, 0.0], [80000.0, 0.0], [100000.0, 0.0], [140000.0, 0.0]], "compile": 1.0, "load": 1.0, "combined_score": -55.975500000000004, "run_success": 1.0}, "complexity": 0.0, "diversity": 0.0, "metadata": {"changes": "Change 1: Replace 52 lines with 47 lines", "parent_metrics": {"policy": "agent", "rates": [50000, 60000, 80000, 100000, 140000], "load_p99_curve": [[50000.0, 5.832], [60000.0, 26.26], [80000.0, 2634.393], [100000.0, 4500.21], [140000.0, 6441.174]], "load_p99_stddev_curve": [[50000.0, 0.0], [60000.0, 0.0], [80000.0, 0.0], [100000.0, 0.0], [140000.0, 0.0]], "compile": 1.0, "load": 1.0, "combined_score": -31694.07, "run_success": 1.0}, "island": 0}, "prompts": null, "artifacts_json": "{\"perf_expl\": \"**Behavior & Performance (precise)**  \\n- P99 latency now tracks the synthetic service time: 2.2\\u202fms at 50\\u202fk rps \\u2192 4.6\\u202fms at 100\\u202fk, rising to 15\\u202fms only once the workers are nearly saturated at 140\\u202fk; the parent policy was already in multi\\u2011second territory from 60\\u202fk upward (results/experiments/exp-20251113-174558/summary.json:1, results/experiments/exp-20251113-165305/summary.json:1).  \\n- Per\\u2011worker stats show \\u22488.1\\u20138.4\\u202fk gets/s at 50\\u202fk and \\u224822\\u201323\\u202fk gets/s at 140\\u202fk for every socket, proving the jittered hash actually sprayed traffic uniformly and eliminated the low-index bias that previously left two workers idle (results/run-20251113-174558/logs/server/server.log:4, results/run-20251113-174718/logs/server/server.log:4).  \\n- Client logs confirm queues stayed shallow (\\u226423 outstanding at 50\\u2013100\\u202fk) and only grew into the hundreds once the offered load neared the 6-worker capacity, so the p99 blow-up at 140\\u202fk is attributable to service-time queueing rather than routing hotspots (results/run-20251113-174558/logs/client.log:2, results/run-20251113-174718/logs/client.log:2).  \\n- Compared to the parent, the new program removes the catastrophic backlog (hundreds of thousands outstanding, missing replies) and delivers two to three orders of magnitude better tail latency at \\u226580\\u202fk rps, solely by ensuring every worker receives work each burst.  \\n- Confidence: **High** \\u2014 both metrics and detailed worker/client logs consistently support the explanation.\\n\\n**Did the behavior achieve the program\\u2019s intent? Why or why not?**  \\n- Intent: mix `reuse->hash` with per-packet randomness, whiten it, and pick exactly one slot so that all active reuseport sockets share work even when only a few UDP flows are present (ebpf/agent.c:34).  \\n- Outcome: yes. Every run shows all six sockets receiving comparable request counts and zero errors, which is exactly what the jittered fmix selector set out to enforce; the remaining latency growth at 140\\u202fk stems from server-side queueing once aggregate throughput nears its compute limit, not from uneven steering (results/run-20251113-174718/logs/server/server.log:4).  \\n- Relative to the parent\\u2014which still pinned traffic to four workers and collapsed at \\u226580\\u202fk\\u2014the new behavior clearly fulfills the fairness/per-core locality goals and thereby restores expected throughput and latency envelopes (results/experiments/exp-20251113-165305/summary.json:1).\\n\\n**Evidence**  \\n- Current load/p99 curve: `results/experiments/exp-20251113-174558/summary.json:1`.  \\n- Parent load/p99 curve: `results/experiments/exp-20251113-165305/summary.json:1`.  \\n- Worker utilization snapshots (even spread): `results/run-20251113-174558/logs/server/server.log:4`, `results/run-20251113-174718/logs/server/server.log:4`.  \\n- Client outstanding/backlog data: `results/run-20251113-174558/logs/client.log:2`, `results/run-20251113-174718/logs/client.log:2`.  \\n- eBPF policy wording and jittered fmix implementation: `ebpf/agent.c:34`.\"}", "artifact_dir": null, "embedding": null}, "parent_metrics": {"policy": "agent", "rates": [50000, 60000, 80000, 100000, 140000], "load_p99_curve": [[50000.0, 2.214], [60000.0, 2.739], [80000.0, 3.31], [100000.0, 4.562], [140000.0, 15.227]], "load_p99_stddev_curve": [[50000.0, 0.0], [60000.0, 0.0], [80000.0, 0.0], [100000.0, 0.0], [140000.0, 0.0]], "compile": 1.0, "load": 1.0, "combined_score": -55.975500000000004, "run_success": 1.0}, "parent_perf_expl": "**Behavior & Performance (precise)**  \n- P99 latency now tracks the synthetic service time: 2.2 ms at 50 k rps → 4.6 ms at 100 k, rising to 15 ms only once the workers are nearly saturated at 140 k; the parent policy was already in multi‑second territory from 60 k upward (results/experiments/exp-20251113-174558/summary.json:1, results/experiments/exp-20251113-165305/summary.json:1).  \n- Per‑worker stats show ≈8.1–8.4 k gets/s at 50 k and ≈22–23 k gets/s at 140 k for every socket, proving the jittered hash actually sprayed traffic uniformly and eliminated the low-index bias that previously left two workers idle (results/run-20251113-174558/logs/server/server.log:4, results/run-20251113-174718/logs/server/server.log:4).  \n- Client logs confirm queues stayed shallow (≤23 outstanding at 50–100 k) and only grew into the hundreds once the offered load neared the 6-worker capacity, so the p99 blow-up at 140 k is attributable to service-time queueing rather than routing hotspots (results/run-20251113-174558/logs/client.log:2, results/run-20251113-174718/logs/client.log:2).  \n- Compared to the parent, the new program removes the catastrophic backlog (hundreds of thousands outstanding, missing replies) and delivers two to three orders of magnitude better tail latency at ≥80 k rps, solely by ensuring every worker receives work each burst.  \n- Confidence: **High** — both metrics and detailed worker/client logs consistently support the explanation.\n\n**Did the behavior achieve the program’s intent? Why or why not?**  \n- Intent: mix `reuse->hash` with per-packet randomness, whiten it, and pick exactly one slot so that all active reuseport sockets share work even when only a few UDP flows are present (ebpf/agent.c:34).  \n- Outcome: yes. Every run shows all six sockets receiving comparable request counts and zero errors, which is exactly what the jittered fmix selector set out to enforce; the remaining latency growth at 140 k stems from server-side queueing once aggregate throughput nears its compute limit, not from uneven steering (results/run-20251113-174718/logs/server/server.log:4).  \n- Relative to the parent—which still pinned traffic to four workers and collapsed at ≥80 k—the new behavior clearly fulfills the fairness/per-core locality goals and thereby restores expected throughput and latency envelopes (results/experiments/exp-20251113-165305/summary.json:1).\n\n**Evidence**  \n- Current load/p99 curve: `results/experiments/exp-20251113-174558/summary.json:1`.  \n- Parent load/p99 curve: `results/experiments/exp-20251113-165305/summary.json:1`.  \n- Worker utilization snapshots (even spread): `results/run-20251113-174558/logs/server/server.log:4`, `results/run-20251113-174718/logs/server/server.log:4`.  \n- Client outstanding/backlog data: `results/run-20251113-174558/logs/client.log:2`, `results/run-20251113-174718/logs/client.log:2`.  \n- eBPF policy wording and jittered fmix implementation: `ebpf/agent.c:34`.", "code_summary": "{\n  \"overview\": {\n    \"scope\": \"Synthetic UDP storage emulator with eBPF-aware load balancing (pebble-server) — only internal/, cmd/, scripts/, workload/, run.sh, run_exp.py included.\",\n    \"languages\": [\n      \"Go 1.22 (internal/*, cmd/*)\",\n      \"Bash (run.sh, scripts/*, workload/run_client.sh)\",\n      \"Python 3 (run_exp.py, scripts/dispatch_docker_experiments.py)\"\n    ],\n    \"artifact_conventions\": {\n      \"logs\": \"logs/<role> (server|client)\",\n      \"results\": \"results/run-* (per run.sh invocation) containing experiment.json + workload_summary.json\",\n      \"eBPF_pin_paths\": \"/sys/fs/bpf/pebble/<RUN_ID> (set by run.sh before launching the Go server)\"\n    }\n  },\n  \"execution_flow\": [\n    {\n      \"order\": 1,\n      \"name\": \"Experiment orchestration\",\n      \"artefact\": \"run_exp.py\",\n      \"description\": \"Thin experiment wrapper: either shells into run.sh for fresh sweeps or parses dispatch manifests to summarise existing runs, then emits summary.json and optional matplotlib plots.\",\n      \"key_steps\": [\n        \"parse_args (run_exp.py:29-95) exposes policies/rates/threads plus manifest + plotting toggles.\",\n        \"invoke_run_sh/load_manifest_runs gather `run-*` directories, extract metrics from workload_summary.json, and pass records into write_experiment_summary/generate_plots.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"order\": 2,\n      \"name\": \"Per-run workflow\",\n      \"artefact\": \"run.sh\",\n      \"description\": \"Convenience script that (a) slices NUMA CPUs between server/client, (b) rebuilds eBPF assets, (c) restarts the Go server for each workload iteration, (d) runs cmd/workload_client under taskset, and (e) aggregates iteration outputs into workload_summary.json.\",\n      \"key_steps\": [\n        \"resolve_cpu_pool/join_by (run.sh:27-118) derive SERVER_CPUSET + CLIENT_CPUSET from CPUSET_POOL or NUMA node 0.\",\n        \"Calls scripts/build_ebpf.sh + scripts/cleanup_ebpf_maps.sh, then orchestrates server via scripts/launch_server.sh and client via run_client wrapper.\",\n        \"Embedded Python (run.sh:415-452) regex-parses client logs into workload_summary.json; final line prints the run directory for downstream tooling.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"order\": 3,\n      \"name\": \"Runtime services\",\n      \"artefacts\": [\n        \"cmd/pebble_server/main.go:21-91 -> internal/server/*\",\n        \"cmd/workload_client/main.go:67-450\"\n      ],\n      \"description\": \"Go binaries that run.sh controls. The server exposes UDP reuseport workers with synthetic latency and optional eBPF selection, while the workload client generates GET/SCAN traffic with latency tracking.\",\n      \"key_steps\": [\n        \"cmd/pebble_server wires CLI flags, creates log/results dirs, enables pprof/profiling, builds server.Config and runs server.New/Run under signal-aware context.\",\n        \"internal/server.Server.New/Run (server.go:104-181) validate config, load eBPF policy, clear pinned maps, then open reuseport sockets, attach eBPF, and spawn workers; handleRequest (server.go:268-325) executes busy-wait delays and records stats.\",\n        \"cmd/workload_client main loop (main.go:67-347) builds UDP payloads, enforces rate limit via golang.org/x/time/rate, tags each request with reqID (makeReqID/parseReqID, main.go:308-337) so receive goroutines can compute latency and feed the aggregator (latencyAggregator, main.go:40-66).\",\n        \"Server stats logger (server.go:328-395) emits per-worker and aggregate lines that scripts/monitoring can scrape.\"\n      ],\n      \"downstream\": [\n        \"Server relies on internal/server/ebpf.go + generated internal/ebpfutil bindings.\",\n        \"Client output is parsed by run.sh’s Python aggregator; logs feed human debugging.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"order\": 4,\n      \"name\": \"Support scripts\",\n      \"artefacts\": [\n        \"scripts/build_ebpf.sh:1-34\",\n        \"scripts/cleanup_ebpf_maps.sh:1-25\",\n        \"scripts/launch_server.sh:1-96\",\n        \"scripts/dispatch_docker_experiments.py:1-654\",\n        \"workload/run_client.sh:1-62\"\n      ],\n      \"description\": \"Reusable helpers for building eBPF assets, cleaning pinned maps, launching the server standalone, dispatching dockerized experiments, and invoking the workload client without run.sh.\",\n      \"key_steps\": [\n        \"build_ebpf.sh enforces clang availability, sets BPF_CLANG/BPF_CFLAGS, adds arch include paths, then runs `go generate ./internal/ebpfutil`.\",\n        \"cleanup_ebpf_maps.sh deletes global pinned maps unless run-specific BPF_PIN_PATH is set, preventing fd leaks between runs.\",\n        \"launch_server.sh builds cmd/pebble_server (unless disabled), enforces single instance via PID file, applies taskset when SERVER_CPUSET is exported, and records PID for run.sh.\",\n        \"dispatch_docker_experiments.py reads NUMA topology (read_numa_cpu_map, line 106), assigns per-container CPU slices, constructs docker run commands (build_jobs, line 149), streams logs asynchronously (run_job, line 221), and optionally post-processes with run_exp.py.\",\n        \"workload/run_client.sh is a thin CLI around `go run ./cmd/workload_client` for ad-hoc manual traffic.\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"modules\": {\n    \"run_exp.py\": {\n      \"high_level_role\": \"Top-level wrapper to sweep policies/rates and collect workload_summary.json files into one experiment summary.\",\n      \"key_points\": [\n        \"parse_args exposes knobs for policies/rates, send-workers, duration, server/client cores, plotting, and manifest reuse.\",\n        \"invoke_run_sh executes run.sh and captures the run directory; load_manifest_runs + link_run_directories reuse docker-dispatched runs.\",\n        \"generate_overall_plot / generate_get_scan_plot optionally render PNGs (skip with --skip-plot).\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"run.sh\": {\n      \"high_level_role\": \"Automation glue that sets up CPU pinning, rebuilds eBPF assets, runs cmd/pebble_server + cmd/workload_client for N iterations, and emits workload_summary.json.\",\n      \"key_points\": [\n        \"resolve_cpu_pool + join_by compute SERVER_CPUSET/CLIENT_CPUSET and export them for both taskset prefixes.\",\n        \"start_server/stop_server wrap scripts/launch_server.sh and enforce clean PID files; run_client pins workload_client under CLIENT_CPUSET.\",\n        \"Embedded Python generates workload_summary.json from logs/workload_iteration_*.txt, which run_exp.py later ingests.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"cmd/pebble_server\": {\n      \"high_level_role\": \"Go binary that instantiates internal/server.Server based on CLI flags and keeps it running with profiling enabled.\",\n      \"key_sections\": [\n        {\n          \"name\": \"Flag wiring & filesystem setup\",\n          \"lines\": \"cmd/pebble_server/main.go:21-52\",\n          \"details\": [\n            \"Defines flags for listen addr, workers, policy, max scan, log/results dirs, timeouts, and synthetic delays; ensures directories exist before logging.\",\n            \"Creates/opens <log-dir>/server.log (append mode) and builds a microsecond-resolution logger.\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"name\": \"Profiling + server lifecycle\",\n          \"lines\": \"cmd/pebble_server/main.go:52-92\",\n          \"details\": [\n            \"Enables block/mutex profiling (runtime.SetBlockProfileRate/SetMutexProfileFraction) and hosts net/http/pprof on 127.0.0.1:6060.\",\n            \"Builds server.Config, calls server.New (fails fast on invalid config or eBPF load issues), installs SIGINT/SIGTERM handler, logs startup configuration, and blocks on srv.Run(ctx).\"\n          ]\n        }\n      ],\n      \"cross_dependencies\": [\n        \"Uses internal/server (Config, Server, eBPF loader).\",\n        \"scripts/launch_server.sh expects -log-dir and -results-dir to exist for log collation.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"cmd/workload_client\": {\n      \"high_level_role\": \"Open-loop UDP workload generator with reqID-based latency measurement and throughput reporting.\",\n      \"key_sections\": [\n        {\n          \"name\": \"Latency data structures\",\n          \"lines\": \"cmd/workload_client/main.go:32-66\",\n          \"details\": [\n            \"latencyRecord and latencyAggregator store per-request latencies, split into overall/GET/SCAN slices; aggregator.totalRecv reports sample counts.\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"name\": \"Main workload loop\",\n          \"lines\": \"cmd/workload_client/main.go:67-307\",\n          \"details\": [\n            \"Parses flags (server, duration, rate, get-frac, scan-limit, key prefix/space, log path, send workers), validates arguments, seeds RNGs per worker.\",\n            \"Creates a shared rate limiter (golang.org/x/time/rate), spawns send goroutines that build payloads (buildPayload, line 363) and assign reqIDs (makeReqID, line 308).\",\n            \"Spawns recv goroutines with deadlines that parse responses, extract reqIDs (parseReqID, line 320), compute latency, and push records into latencyCh.\",\n            \"Aggregator + monitor goroutines track outstanding requests and total received counts.\"\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"name\": \"Statistics reporting\",\n          \"lines\": \"cmd/workload_client/main.go:308-451\",\n          \"details\": [\n            \"Helper functions (makeReqID/parseReqID/writeHex/parseHex) ensure both sides share the encoding.\",\n            \"percentile/average/reportLatencyStats (lines 376-451) sort copies of the latency slices, compute p50/p90/p99/avg, and print textual summaries that run.sh’s regex parser expects.\"\n          ]\n        }\n      ],\n      \"cross_dependencies\": [\n        \"run.sh’s run_client wrapper invokes this with -log pointing at logs/client.log; workload/run_client.sh uses go run for convenience.\",\n        \"Server assumes GET payload format 'GET key [reqID]' and SCAN 'SCAN start limit [reqID]' as implemented here.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"internal/server\": {\n      \"high_level_role\": \"Core server runtime, including configuration validation, UDP worker pool, request parser, stats logging, and eBPF policy integration.\",\n      \"key_files\": [\n        {\n          \"path\": \"internal/server/config.go\",\n          \"summary\": \"Config struct + Validate clamp runtime knobs to sane defaults so CLI/script errors don’t crash the server.\",\n          \"key_functions\": [\n            {\n              \"name\": \"Config.Validate\",\n              \"lines\": \"internal/server/config.go:19-43\",\n              \"details\": [\n                \"Sets ListenAddr default (127.0.0.1:9000), ensures at least one worker, caps MaxScanKeys >= 1 (default 100k), enforces non-zero timeouts/delays.\",\n                \"Called by server.New before any socket/eBPF work, giving run.sh freedom to pass zero/negative placeholder values.\"\n              ]\n            }\n          ]\n        },\n        {\n          \"path\": \"internal/server/server.go\",\n          \"summary\": \"Implements the Server struct, worker lifecy